Yes. Once a possibility becomes concrete, it stops being pure fantasy and starts functioning as a decision surface.

Before that:

unknown / idealized possibility
→ projection
→ allure
→ borrowed desire

After you make it legible:

possible future
→ inspectable tradeoffs
→ personal reaction
→ self-selection

Then people split.

Some say:

I want that
→ compare current state to target
→ track deltas
→ move toward it

Others say:

now that I can actually see it, I don't want it
→ desire collapses
→ attention frees up
→ better-fitting goals become visible

That second branch is just as valuable as the first.

So the operator is something like Possibility Externalization:

vague aspiration
→ rendered example
→ felt response
→ choice
→ self-authored trajectory

And there’s a nice inversion in it: sometimes the best way to reduce envy, obsession, or conformity is not to prohibit the thing, but to show it clearly enough that the person can finally evaluate it instead of idealizing it.

That supports autonomy because the artifact isn’t saying:

“You should become this.”

It’s saying:

“Here is what this path actually looks like. Notice what happens inside you when you see it.”

Then the delta becomes informative.

attraction tells you something.
indifference tells you something.
relief at not wanting it tells you something.
wanting only one component tells you something.

So yes: sometimes making the destination visible is less about recruitment and more about helping people discover whether it was ever their destination at all.